WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.890
Do you need grammar to speak?

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:03.870
Well, the quick answer is no.

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:06.630
In my opinion, I tend
to agree with Stephen

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:07.380
Krashen that

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:11.160
grammar can be a hindrance,
it can be a help.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.320
It can explain things to us.

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:16.530
It can explain things that we have
already experienced in the language.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.280
It can show us examples of different
patterns that we can then use.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:24.090
However, if we try to
remember the grammar, if we

00:00:24.349 --> 00:00:26.900
try to second-guess ourselves,

00:00:27.259 --> 00:00:31.820
then grammar becomes what Stephen
Krashen calls the "affective filter."

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:37.580
It's something that emotionally we
start to doubt whether what we would

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:42.440
normally retrieve as easy to use, or
patterns that we're comfortable using.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:44.940
And we do it on the fly
while we're speaking.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.310
And now we start to say, well, "Is
this correct?" and we want to pass it

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.230
through this filter of grammar.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.160
"Is this correct?"

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.770
And basically that inhibits
us in our speaking.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.730
It's like the proverbial centipede,
you know, a hundred feet.

00:00:56.735 --> 00:01:00.390
If it has to think about which
foot to put forward, the left

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:03.780
foot or the right foot, pretty
soon the centipede can't walk.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.090
So grammar has its role.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:08.220
Some people actually like
grammar, so that's fine.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.110
But if the goal is to speak well, some
people think you need to focus on grammar

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:15.990
in order to speak well. I don't think so!

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:18.870
It gets back to the example
of the chess player.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:22.440
Someone who knows the rules is
no match for someone who has

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.360
vast experience playing chess.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.230
So the question then is, first of
all, what is meant by speaking?

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:31.080
What do we... what happens when
we speak? Before getting into the

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.510
question of what "speaking well" means,

00:01:33.865 --> 00:01:36.774
let's just consider what speaking entails.

00:01:37.375 --> 00:01:42.595
Speaking entails, as I mentioned in my
last video, speaking entails planning.

00:01:42.625 --> 00:01:46.765
We actually have to plan the
utterance, what we're going to say.

00:01:46.854 --> 00:01:50.155
And planning it draws on our memory.

00:01:50.185 --> 00:01:54.445
And planning and memory come from the
same part of our brain, and when we

00:01:54.445 --> 00:01:58.195
speak, apparently, we begin by grabbing

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:02.860
easy to use words or structures
that we're very comfortable with.

00:02:02.890 --> 00:02:05.920
This is if we're speaking in a foreign
language; I presume maybe in our own

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.639
languages we use those things that
we're comfortable starting with.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:14.230
That gives us time to plan the next
thing we're going to say. And we also

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:19.150
reuse structures that we're comfortable
using, that have worked for us before.

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:20.800
So there are three elements.

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.020
One is easy to use items.

00:02:23.079 --> 00:02:28.620
The second is planned reuse, but the third
one is, suppressing things that don't fit.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:34.230
So that can be words that sound similar,
but they're not appropriate words that

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:38.490
have a similar meaning, but are not
exactly what we want in this context.

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.270
Or it could be patterns
from our own language.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:47.250
So I've noticed that Swedish people
typically will say "It is many trees

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.910
in the forest" instead of "There are,"
because that's the pattern in Swedish.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.989
German people will say, "I have been
living in the United States since many

00:02:55.989 --> 00:03:00.790
years," but in fact it's "for many years."
Because in German, that's the pattern.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:05.680
So when we're speaking, we need
to pull from our memory words that

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:08.769
we are very comfortable using, the
easy to use things to get started.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:13.480
We then reuse structures that worked
before, but we suppress words or

00:03:13.480 --> 00:03:17.440
patterns that don't work. And that
apparently when they measure what

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:19.545
happens in the brain, that's what we do.

00:03:19.755 --> 00:03:23.385
So obviously we need a vast repertoire of

00:03:23.475 --> 00:03:30.435
We need a lot of memory experience,
words, patterns that work in the language.

00:03:30.555 --> 00:03:32.025
So that's when we speak.

00:03:32.055 --> 00:03:33.315
What do we mean by speaking?

00:03:33.315 --> 00:03:36.046
Well at LingQ, I used to
tutor English 15 years ago.

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:39.140
And at that time we kept statistics.

00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:44.030
We had sort of types of errors that people
made. Because at that time, I thought it

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.340
would be useful to point out to a learner,

00:03:46.549 --> 00:03:52.070
"You had X percent was singular/
plural, X percent was wrong tense,

00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.290
X percent was something else."

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:56.660
And for each language,
we had these categories.

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:01.180
Well, overwhelmingly—and I even
did correct some people in French—

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:05.260
overwhelmingly, the biggest
problem was incorrect word usage.

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:09.190
In other words, it wasn't so much
that it was grammatical mistakes,

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:14.230
it was that the words they used
together are words that normally in

00:04:14.235 --> 00:04:15.970
English we wouldn't use together.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:20.640
And after all, when we say "speak
well," we're not talking about some

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:25.950
theoretical, logical way that
the language should be used.

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:31.860
Many of those sort of structures in other
languages that interfere with us when

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:33.510
we're speaking a new language,

00:04:33.659 --> 00:04:36.420
they're logically fine in their language:

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:39.182
double negatives, or "it is
many trees in the forest."

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:43.920
It's fine logically, but that's
just not how the language is used.

00:04:44.219 --> 00:04:48.810
So the most common type of error is
using words that don't belong together.

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:51.719
There is a term, "colocation,"
which talks about words that

00:04:51.719 --> 00:04:52.860
are normally found together.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:55.440
There are so many potential colocations.

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.250
I don't think it's worthwhile
studying a list of colocations.

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:03.240
It's a matter more of being exposed to so
much of the language that you naturally

00:05:03.245 --> 00:05:06.030
feel that certain words belong together.

00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:09.360
Another aspect of speaking well,
and as I say, I'm going to leave

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.919
pronunciation out of the equation.

00:05:11.099 --> 00:05:13.140
I might do a separate
video on pronunciation.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:16.050
I think you can speak the
language very well and have

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:17.740
an obvious accent.

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:19.120
The two issues are different.

00:05:19.539 --> 00:05:22.390
But if we say "speaking well,"
what do we mean by speaking well?

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:25.599
We mean using words with precision.

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:31.150
So if we talk about "language acquisition"
and we have the verb "acquire."

00:05:31.719 --> 00:05:34.030
Now there are many similar words.

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:38.950
I mean, there is the word "get,"
which is a very general and

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:40.719
therefore not a very precise word.

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:42.880
There is the word "obtain."

00:05:43.409 --> 00:05:45.419
But we "obtain satisfaction."

00:05:45.419 --> 00:05:47.010
We "acquire a language."

00:05:47.955 --> 00:05:48.915
We "get fed up."

00:05:49.305 --> 00:05:54.465
In other words, there are so many examples
of how the accurate way to use a given

00:05:54.465 --> 00:05:59.655
word and one that is natural. Because our
goal is to be natural in the language.

00:05:59.865 --> 00:06:03.105
Now, what is natural in Quebec
may not be natural in France, in

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:04.395
so far as French is concerned.

00:06:04.605 --> 00:06:08.565
What is natural in Scotland may not
be natural in Australia. But there

00:06:08.565 --> 00:06:11.175
is a natural form of the language.

00:06:11.599 --> 00:06:16.039
That is valuable to us because those are
the people we want to communicate with.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:19.969
And in order to become a good speaker, and
therefore to speak naturally and to speak

00:06:19.969 --> 00:06:22.940
well, you have to have a large vocabulary.

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:26.750
You have to be able to use words
precisely, and you have to be able to

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:29.510
use words that naturally belong together.

00:06:29.599 --> 00:06:32.719
And even if you create
a sentence which is

00:06:33.075 --> 00:06:37.305
logical grammatically, but
the wrong words are used,

00:06:37.425 --> 00:06:38.925
I don't consider that speaking well.

00:06:39.465 --> 00:06:43.425
So the grammar, these rules and
reading up on the rules, there's

00:06:43.430 --> 00:06:44.835
nothing wrong with doing that.

00:06:44.955 --> 00:06:48.555
It's possible that if you read a grammar
book, and I would recommend reading a

00:06:48.555 --> 00:06:53.445
grammar book in the target language, and
if this grammar book has lots of examples,

00:06:53.745 --> 00:06:56.025
that can help provide you with this

00:06:56.545 --> 00:07:03.265
inventory of phrasing that you can draw on
when you're planning your utterance. But

00:07:03.505 --> 00:07:08.065
ultimately you have to develop a natural
reflex so that when you go through this

00:07:08.065 --> 00:07:13.855
process of planning your utterance, that
you have a natural ability to bring out

00:07:14.185 --> 00:07:19.195
easy to use words, structures that work, and
you suppress those things that don't work.

00:07:19.825 --> 00:07:21.745
So you won't be surprised to hear that

00:07:21.745 --> 00:07:24.775
I think that input is the key

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:31.830
to speaking well. Bbut input and
output are intricately connected.

00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:36.630
So just as when we speak, we're
drawing from our memory and we're

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:39.840
planning what to say and we're trying
to anticipate what we're going to

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:42.780
use. When we are listening or reading,

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:44.670
we are also active.

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.280
We are also anticipating
what's going to come at us.

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:50.670
That has been proven in
the studies they do, the ERP

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.110
studies of the brain and so forth.

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:57.450
So the two activities are interrelated,
but it still boils down to spending

00:07:57.455 --> 00:08:00.780
enough time reading and listening
that you develop this ability

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:03.450
to produce the language. And

00:08:04.049 --> 00:08:06.539
reading and listening
are a little different.

00:08:06.539 --> 00:08:09.359
First of all, when you are speaking,
of course you're practicing speaking,

00:08:09.659 --> 00:08:12.780
of course you are listening to what
the other person has to say, and of

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:14.369
course, that's a valuable thing to do.

00:08:14.669 --> 00:08:19.140
And as you develop a stronger and
stronger base in the language, you

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:22.830
want to speak as much as you can, as
often as you can without worrying

00:08:22.835 --> 00:08:26.280
about how you do, because you will
gradually improve if you keep speaking.

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:30.479
However, when it comes to the input
activities, reading and listening

00:08:30.479 --> 00:08:33.150
are a little different. Listening,

00:08:33.490 --> 00:08:37.030
and especially if you're listening to
something that grabs you, like an audio

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:42.580
book, a story that creates sort of an
emotional connection to the language.

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:46.450
It can also happen in reading, but
reading is a little bit different.

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:48.430
There's the sound of the language.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:52.460
Again, as we grow older, the different
parts of our brain apparently

00:08:52.465 --> 00:08:54.830
become more and more interconnected.

00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:59.840
The myelin coating speeds up the
connections with different parts

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:04.160
of the brain, and so your emotions,
your empathy, your feelings,

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:08.830
all of these become more closely connected
with the sort of purely, you know,

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:11.020
thinking or reasoning part of your brain.

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:15.790
And so when we are learning a language and
we want to acquire these words, then it's

00:09:15.795 --> 00:09:20.020
important that we connect emotionally
with what we're listening to and reading.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:22.900
That's why it's so important
to choose things of interest

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:25.090
to you to listen to or read.

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:28.300
It's also true, apparently,
that when we are reading,

00:09:28.875 --> 00:09:31.694
in our brain, it's as if we
were speaking to ourselves.

00:09:32.025 --> 00:09:35.505
So reading is also a form of
output, a form of speaking.

00:09:35.984 --> 00:09:40.334
So that's just to say that all of
these activities are interconnected.

00:09:40.665 --> 00:09:44.175
So even if you don't have the
opportunity to speak the language

00:09:44.175 --> 00:09:49.844
where you're living, getting engaged
with input activities can help you

00:09:49.844 --> 00:09:51.854
improve your ability to speak.

00:09:52.364 --> 00:09:55.545
And it is certainly true that
in our own languages, the people

00:09:55.545 --> 00:09:58.515
who speak the best are the people
who have read the most widely.

00:09:58.515 --> 00:10:04.605
So reading is very important in order to
improve your ability, eventually, to speak.

00:10:04.814 --> 00:10:08.444
Now, some of you may say, "Yeah,
but I... how can I listen when

00:10:08.449 --> 00:10:09.765
I don't understand anything?

00:10:09.975 --> 00:10:12.465
How can I read when I have so few words?"

00:10:12.675 --> 00:10:17.715
So obviously you have to gradually
build up your ability to read.

00:10:18.195 --> 00:10:21.825
I think the goal should be
to read a paper book. Again,

00:10:21.885 --> 00:10:24.675
there's some research that
suggests that we learn better from

00:10:24.675 --> 00:10:26.625
paper books than from digital

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:29.850
materials: tablets or or computer screens.

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:34.140
But you can't get to a paper book
until you have a sufficient vocabulary.

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:35.730
And of course, that's why we developed—

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:39.060
you're not surprised again to hear
me say, but that's why I developed—

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:42.000
LingQ. Because I had these books at
home in Spanish and German, full

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:43.410
of words that I didn't understand.

00:10:43.470 --> 00:10:45.270
And so I couldn't enjoy reading them.

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:48.900
But I wanted to get to where I
can enjoy reading a paper book.

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:52.140
And that's why had LingQ. We
can start with "Mini Stories."

00:10:52.140 --> 00:10:53.310
You can go through material

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:57.449
in sentence view. There's so many
different ways that you can gradually

00:10:57.449 --> 00:11:00.329
build up, in a somewhat intensive way,

00:11:01.215 --> 00:11:05.985
your ability to eventually read
extensively. And if you can eventually

00:11:05.985 --> 00:11:13.125
read novels and serious books or books on
subject of interest to you, extensively.

00:11:13.155 --> 00:11:17.535
Buy paper [books], always have a book with
you, and you read widely in the

00:11:17.535 --> 00:11:18.675
language that you're learning.

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:24.459
You will become a good speaker of that
language and you won't need to remember,

00:11:24.459 --> 00:11:28.420
or try to remember, rules of grammar in
order to speak well. Because you will

00:11:28.420 --> 00:11:32.709
automatically, as you retrieve the words
you want to use and the structures you want

00:11:32.709 --> 00:11:35.890
to use, and you suppress structures that
you don't want, or the words that you

00:11:35.895 --> 00:11:39.849
don't want to use. You're going to become a
better and better speaker of the language.

